  "query_preview": "..\n\nCopious amounts of upper level energy over central Canada will lead \nto a pronounced upper level trough over the Great Lakes and \nparticularly over New England on Thursday. The 00z DTX Raob revealed \na 500 MB Height of 574 DAM. Heights look to fall and bottom out \naround 558 DAM over central sections of the CWA Thursday morning, \nper 00z Euro. Today will be a mild day ahead of a mostly dry cold \nfront tracking through. An 850 MB thermal ridge of 12 to 15 C \ntranslate through during the mid-day, but mixing depths more than \nlikely come up short, maintaining highs well into the 70s, supported \nby 925 MB temps of 17 to 18 C. An upper level jet streak racing \nthrough northern lower Michigan offers up a slight chance of showers \nacross the northern half of the CWA, as 700/500 MB cold pools (-6 C/-\n24) track across. Mid level lapse rates will be steep, and moisture \nenhancement from Saginaw Bay/Lake Huron certainly seems sufficient, \nwith even isolate thunderstorms possible over Lake Huron with capes \naoa 500 J/kg. \n\nA cool-down is expected to end workweek as 850 MB temps bottom out \nat 3 to 4 C Thursday night. There is some concern for temps to reach \ninto the low to mid 30s, although with the center of high pressure \nover the Ohio Valley, winds may fail to fully decouple, keeping mins \nelevated in the upper 30s to lower 40s. \n\nAn active weather pattern as we head into the weekend. A strong warm \nfront over the Central Plains/Midwest Thursday lifts through the \nGreat Lakes regions Friday night. A low level jet core (45+ knots) \nlooks to track through northern Lower Michigan, with the showalter \nindex only at zero or slightly negative. Thus, euro ensembles \nsuggesting just a 30 to 40 percent chance of showers. Outgoing NBM \npops are below 14 percent, and probably ok as long as the jet core \ntracks farther north. \n\nReally impressive warm air/ridging building in on Saturday ahead of \nthe soon-to-be-named TS Isaias coming out of the western Gulf Coast \nstates Saturday night. 850 MB temps advertised to reach near 20 C \nover Lower Michigan Saturday evening. High temperatures aoa 80 \ndegrees come into play, per euro ensembles. Record highs for the day \nreside in the mid 80s for reference. Based on the pressure \ngradient/pattern, looks like Tri-Cities/Flint Vicinity are set up \nfor the warmest readings.\n\nThe second half of the weekend into Monday could be very wet, as the \nhurricane center official track brings the remnants of the tropical \nsystem into the central Ohio Valley by Sunday evening, in line with \nthe Euro solution. NBM pops are taking a more conservative approach \nfor Sunday through Sunday night. Still, there are too many variables \n(such as strength, areal size, and potential deflection to the east \nby the time it reaches this latitude) this far out to make the call \none way or the other.",
